\vspace{-0.4em}
\section{Conclusion and Limitations}
\vspace{-0.3em}

\textbf{Diagnosis.} We identified \emph{normalization-induced routing collapse}: MoE adapters on instance-normalized time series foundation models collapse to a single expert because the same per-window normalization that handles distribution shift also strips the $(\mu,\sigma)$ statistics the router needs to distinguish input regimes. Observation~\ref{thm:mi_decomp} formalizes the loss as a mutual-information gap; the signal-ratio proxy $R(\mathcal{D})$ predicts where it bites at Spearman $\rho{=}{-}0.88$ ($p{<}0.002$); and eight causal controls (Table~\ref{tab:causal_controls}) jointly rule out optimization, geometry, and routing-paradigm explanations.

\textbf{Three fixes share one ingredient.} Three architecturally distinct interventions, all giving the router or the experts access to the raw pre-normalization input, confirm the diagnosis: RR-MoA wins $54/54$ cells against the best fixed adapter ($26$--$79\%$ MSE); SR-MoA drops the external router entirely and outperforms RR-MoA by $13$--$42\%$; Residual-IA\textsuperscript{+} extends the principle to the expert level and closes the long-standing gap to DLinear on $107/123$ cross-backbone cells. The mechanism is modality-general (replicated on ResNet/ViT MoE with InstanceNorm on CIFAR-10, Appendix~\ref{app:vision_moe}) and absent from LayerNorm-only backbones (Timer-XL, raw vs.\ hidden routing differ by only $4$--$9\%$, $15$--$100{\times}$ smaller than on MOMENT), so the normalization \emph{type}, not the modality or the architecture, is the relevant axis.

\textbf{Limitations.} The diagnosis is specific to per-window normalizers that strip $(\mu,\sigma)$ from the input (RevIN, BatchNorm1d, GroupNorm, vision InstanceNorm); LayerNorm- and RMSNorm-only backbones exhibit no collapse and no fix is needed. $R(\mathcal{D})$ is a tractable monotone proxy rather than a sharp decision threshold: it correctly flags Traffic ($R{=}0.14$) as a boundary case, but predicting which intermediate $R$ values benefit at which freeze level remains open. Empirical evaluation covers forecasting and imputation; classification and anomaly detection remain to be tested. The fix presumes raw pre-normalization input is accessible at routing time, which holds for adapter-style finetuning of TSFMs but not for purely API-served foundation models. Our expert pool is also a fixed set of five hand-coded topologies; whether learned expert pools amplify or attenuate the gain is open.

\textbf{Outlook.} The signal-ratio diagnostic is portable: any future MoE-adapter design on a normalized backbone can compute $R(\mathcal{D})$ on the target distribution before training to predict whether raw routing is required. Surfacing the upstream architectural cause of routing collapse, rather than treating it as an optimization symptom to be patched with load balancing or z-loss, widens the design space of MoE adapters that have so far been blocked by ineffective rescue mechanisms.\label{page:end_main}

